1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,866
Aarav's tiny speaker plays loud music
all evening on one small battery.

2
00:00:05,166 --> 00:00:09,033
But the old lab amplifier gets
hot, even when it plays nothing.

3
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:10,233
Same job.

4
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:12,263
So why does one waste so much power?

5
00:00:12,563 --> 00:00:15,672
The secret is two
transistors taking turns.

6
00:00:15,672 --> 00:00:17,304
A push-pull amplifier.

7
00:00:17,804 --> 00:00:21,280
Think of a big saw with two
people, one at each end.

8
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:23,908
One person pulls the saw towards herself.

9
00:00:24,258 --> 00:00:26,406
Then the other person pulls it back.

10
00:00:26,756 --> 00:00:30,222
Each one works half the time,
and rests the other half.

11
00:00:30,872 --> 00:00:33,769
An ordinary class A
amplifier is different.

12
00:00:33,769 --> 00:00:35,403
It has one transistor.

13
00:00:35,753 --> 00:00:39,816
That transistor stays half on
all the time, even in silence.

14
00:00:40,166 --> 00:00:43,912
So current flows and heat
is made, even with no music.

15
00:00:44,262 --> 00:00:47,714
A class B push-pull amplifier
has two transistors.

16
00:00:48,064 --> 00:00:49,934
In silence, both are off.

17
00:00:49,934 --> 00:00:51,598
Almost no current flows.

18
00:00:51,598 --> 00:00:52,858
Nothing gets hot.

19
00:00:53,508 --> 00:00:55,425
Let's build it on the breadboard.

20
00:00:55,775 --> 00:01:00,058
An input transformer splits the
signal into two opposite halves.

21
00:01:00,408 --> 00:01:04,050
Its middle wire, called the
centre tap, goes to ground.

22
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:08,540
Two n p n transistors, Q
one on top, Q two below.

23
00:01:08,890 --> 00:01:12,761
An output transformer joins their
work and drives the speaker.

24
00:01:13,111 --> 00:01:14,648
Now a wave comes in.

25
00:01:14,648 --> 00:01:16,324
Watch the positive half.

26
00:01:16,674 --> 00:01:17,754
Q one turns on.

27
00:01:17,754 --> 00:01:20,122
Electrons flow through the top half only.

28
00:01:20,472 --> 00:01:22,456
Then the negative half comes.

29
00:01:22,456 --> 00:01:23,884
Q one switches off.

30
00:01:24,234 --> 00:01:28,244
Now Q two turns on, and current
flows through the bottom half.

31
00:01:28,594 --> 00:01:30,526
Q one pushes, Q two pulls.

32
00:01:30,526 --> 00:01:32,708
That is why we call it push-pull.

33
00:01:33,058 --> 00:01:36,656
On the scope, the output is
one smooth, complete wave.

34
00:01:37,306 --> 00:01:39,391
Let's look at the two currents one by one.

35
00:01:39,741 --> 00:01:42,007
Q one carries only the positive humps.

36
00:01:42,357 --> 00:01:44,683
Q two carries only the negative humps.

37
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:48,393
Each transistor conducts for
one hundred and eighty degrees.

38
00:01:48,393 --> 00:01:49,350
Half a cycle.

39
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:53,903
The output transformer adds both
halves back into one full wave.

40
00:01:54,553 --> 00:01:56,651
Two transistors, taking turns.

41
00:01:56,651 --> 00:01:58,720
Each one rests half the time.

42
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,403
But there is one small problem.

43
00:02:01,753 --> 00:02:06,350
A silicon transistor needs about zero
point seven volts before it turns

44
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:07,250
on.

45
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:11,017
So near zero, the input is too
small for both transistors.

46
00:02:11,367 --> 00:02:13,500
For a moment, neither conducts.

47
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:15,042
The output stays flat.

48
00:02:15,392 --> 00:02:18,570
This kink in the wave is
called crossover distortion.

49
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:20,793
The fix is simple.

50
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:23,758
Give both bases a tiny forward voltage.

51
00:02:24,108 --> 00:02:26,068
Often two diodes do this job.

52
00:02:26,068 --> 00:02:28,124
Now each transistor is just on.

53
00:02:28,474 --> 00:02:29,797
The kink disappears.

54
00:02:29,797 --> 00:02:32,610
This slightly on version
is called class AB.

55
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:34,942
Now, how efficient is it?

56
00:02:34,942 --> 00:02:36,588
One small step at a time.

57
00:02:36,938 --> 00:02:41,565
Efficiency is the useful output
power, divided by the power taken from

58
00:02:41,565 --> 00:02:42,539
the supply.

59
00:02:43,239 --> 00:02:47,511
The output power is V p
times I p, divided by two.

60
00:02:48,211 --> 00:02:50,620
The supply gives a series of humps.

61
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:52,990
Their average is two I p over pi.

62
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:58,060
So the supply power is
VCC, times two I p over pi.

63
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,085
Divide the two.

64
00:03:00,085 --> 00:03:04,261
Efficiency equals pi over
four, times V p over VCC.

65
00:03:04,961 --> 00:03:08,397
At full output, V p almost equals VCC.

66
00:03:09,097 --> 00:03:11,559
Then the efficiency is pi over four.

67
00:03:11,559 --> 00:03:13,751
Seventy eight point five percent.

68
00:03:14,401 --> 00:03:19,446
A transformer coupled class A amplifier
reaches only fifty percent at best.

69
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:21,056
Worked example.

70
00:03:21,056 --> 00:03:23,584
How much of each wave is lost to the kink?

71
00:03:23,934 --> 00:03:26,661
The input peak is one point four volts.

72
00:03:26,661 --> 00:03:29,265
Each transistor needs zero point seven.

73
00:03:29,615 --> 00:03:32,210
The input is V p times sine theta.

74
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:36,287
It reaches zero point seven
when sine theta is one half.

75
00:03:36,287 --> 00:03:37,780
That is thirty degrees.

76
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,946
So the output is flat from minus
thirty to plus thirty degrees.

77
00:03:41,946 --> 00:03:43,283
That is sixty degrees.

78
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:46,095
A full cycle crosses zero twice.

79
00:03:46,095 --> 00:03:49,438
So sixty times two is one
hundred and twenty degrees.

80
00:03:50,138 --> 00:03:52,932
One hundred and twenty out of three sixty.

81
00:03:52,932 --> 00:03:55,074
One third of the wave is lost.

82
00:03:55,724 --> 00:03:57,186
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:03:57,536 --> 00:04:01,196
Many students write that each
transistor conducts for the full cycle.

84
00:04:01,546 --> 00:04:02,446
Wrong.

85
00:04:02,088 --> 00:04:06,196
In class B, each one conducts for
only one hundred and eighty degrees.

86
00:04:06,846 --> 00:04:11,661
And seventy eight point five percent
is the best case, at full output only.

87
00:04:12,011 --> 00:04:13,632
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:13,632 --> 00:04:15,492
In PUC board papers.

89
00:04:16,142 --> 00:04:20,514
Draw the push-pull circuit, explain
how it works, and explain crossover

90
00:04:20,514 --> 00:04:21,414
distortion.

91
00:04:22,016 --> 00:04:22,916
Try this.

92
00:04:22,829 --> 00:04:27,113
A class B push-pull amplifier takes
twenty five watts from the supply,

93
00:04:27,113 --> 00:04:28,739
at its best efficiency.

94
00:04:28,739 --> 00:04:32,001
How much power turns into
heat in the transistors?

95
00:04:37,401 --> 00:04:38,301
B.

96
00:04:37,809 --> 00:04:41,690
About five point four watts,
shared by the two transistors.

97
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:45,740
At best, the efficiency is
seventy eight point five percent.

98
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:50,202
Useful output is zero point seven
eight five times twenty five.

99
00:04:50,202 --> 00:04:52,329
About nineteen point six watts.

100
00:04:52,679 --> 00:04:54,074
The rest is heat.

101
00:04:54,074 --> 00:04:58,625
Twenty five minus nineteen point
six is about five point four watts.

102
00:04:59,275 --> 00:05:00,821
Screenshot this card.

103
00:05:04,021 --> 00:05:06,406
Next, positive and negative feedback.

104
00:05:06,406 --> 00:05:08,801
Why does a microphone suddenly scream?
